std::isnan/isinf/isfinite overloads for Eigen::half and bfloat16 on QNX libc++

QNX's libc++ (from SDP 8.0) declares std::isnan, std::isinf and
std::isfinite only as templates constrained to arithmetic types; there are
no float/double overloads that a type converting to float could use.
Eigen::half and Eigen::bfloat16 are such types, and the complex-number
comparisons (include/complex_types.h) and integer casts (_src/intn_numpy.h)
call std::isnan on them: "no matching function for call to
'isnan(Eigen::half&)'". libstdc++ and newer libc++ accept it. Add the
overloads for the two Eigen types, forwarding to Eigen::numext, the way
ml_dtypes already provides std:: overloads for its own float8 types.

Upstream-Status: QNX-specific

--- a/ml_dtypes/_src/common.h
+++ b/ml_dtypes/_src/common.h
@@ -28,6 +28,21 @@
 
 #include "Eigen/Core"
 
+#if defined(__QNX__) && defined(_LIBCPP_VERSION)
+// QNX's libc++ declares std::isnan/isinf/isfinite only as templates
+// constrained to arithmetic types, with no float/double overloads that
+// Eigen::half and Eigen::bfloat16 could convert to. Give them overloads,
+// as ml_dtypes does for its own types (include/float8.h).
+namespace std {
+inline bool isnan(const Eigen::half& a) { return Eigen::numext::isnan(a); }
+inline bool isinf(const Eigen::half& a) { return Eigen::numext::isinf(a); }
+inline bool isfinite(const Eigen::half& a) { return Eigen::numext::isfinite(a); }
+inline bool isnan(const Eigen::bfloat16& a) { return Eigen::numext::isnan(a); }
+inline bool isinf(const Eigen::bfloat16& a) { return Eigen::numext::isinf(a); }
+inline bool isfinite(const Eigen::bfloat16& a) { return Eigen::numext::isfinite(a); }
+}  // namespace std
+#endif
+
 namespace ml_dtypes {
 
 using half = Eigen::half;
